<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Beylik of Tunis</h1><p class="st-meta">1705 CE – 1956 CE · capital Tunis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tunisia under the Husainid beys, autonomous within the Ottoman world, which issued the Arab world's first constitution before falling under French rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1705, during an invasion from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers</a>, the cavalry officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husainid-dynasty">Husainid dynasty</a>. Succeeding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ottoman-tunisia">Ottoman Tunisia</a>, the beys acknowledged Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> but ruled as hereditary princes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammuda-ibn-ali">Hammuda Pasha</a>, ruling from 1782 to 1814, brought prosperity and independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-i-bey">Ahmad Bey</a> modernised the army and abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1846. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fundamental-pact">Fundamental Pact</a> of 1857 and the constitution of 1861 limited the bey's power, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khayr-al-din-al-tunisi">Khayr al-Din</a> pursued reforms. But heavy debts led to an international financial commission in 1869.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invaded in 1881, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bardo">Treaty of Bardo</a> created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-protectorate-of-tunisia">French protectorate of Tunisia</a>. The beys reigned under French control until independence in 1956, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-amin-bey">Muhammad al-Amin Bey</a> became king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tunisia">Kingdom of Tunisia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/beylik-of-tunis.jpg" alt="Beylik of Tunis" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file: · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Beylik_of_Tunis_(1831%E2%80%931881)_and_Tunisia_(1881%E2%80%931959).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1705 CE – 1956 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tunis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Husayn ibn Ali, Ahmad Bey, Muhammad al-Amin Bey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Ottoman Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Regency of Tunis, Husainid Tunisia, Husainid dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1830&amp;focus=beylik-of-tunis" aria-label="Beylik of Tunis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4935 851 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Beylik of Tunis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5255 1107L5255 1107M5255 1107L5709 970M5255 1107L5077 1108M5255 1107L5255 1107"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5255 1107h0M5709 970h0M5077 1108h0M5255 1107h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5255 1107h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5255 1107h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tunis. <a href="/atlas?year=1830&amp;focus=beylik-of-tunis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
